"Floe: Federated Specialization for Real-Time LLM–SLM Inference (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.14302v1)\nciteturn31907view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.14302v1\",\"lineno\":150}); Total lines: 591\nL136: Figure cite112†6 presents a heatmap that visualizes the similarity of task embeddings across different tasks. The results indicate that embeddings within the same domain exhibit higher similarity, highlighting the presence of data heterogeneity across different tasks.\nL137: This underscores the need for Floe to reconcile two priorities: (1) leveraging cross-domain commonalities through federated learning, and (2) accommodating device-specific characteristics via personalization, thereby mitigating data heterogeneity’s impact on model performance and efficiency.\nL138: cite113†Image: Refer to caption Fig. 5: Task embedding heatmap.\nL139: \nL140: cite114†Image: Refer to caption Fig. 6: Workflow of Floe fine-tuning process.\nL141: #### II-B 3 System heterogeneity\nL142: arising from performance and efficiency variations across diverse hardware platforms, is especially pronounced in edge environments with thousands of distinct System-on-Chips that exhibit heterogeneous compute and storage capabilities. Profiling results (Figure cite115†4 ) reveal three observations: (1) hardware disparities significantly affect latency; (2) foreground workloads exacerbate runtime variance; and (3) LoRA rank selection introduces non-trivial computational trade-offs.\nL143: These findings motivate our heterogeneity-aware rank adaptation. Traditional approaches [cite116†62 , cite103†54 , cite117†63 ] address system heterogeneity through device selection strategies but fail to reduce per-device training overhead. For LLMs, high memory demands further restrict eligible device pools.\nL144: Recent methods [cite118†64 , cite119†65 ] propose dynamic model compression: ZeroFL [cite118†64 ] partitions weights into active/inactive subsets for inference and sparse updates during backpropagation, while FedMef [cite119†65 ] preserves post-pruning accuracy via budget-aware parameter extrusion. However, as discussed in Section cite12†II-B1 , compression introduces additional computational overhead, degrades domain-specific knowledge, and undermines privacy through intermediate parameter exposure.\nL145: In contrast, SLMs mitigate these challenges by streamlining architectures and training data to focus on core task requirements. This motivates Floe to develop an adaptive heterogeneity-aware mechanism that dynamically accommodates system heterogeneity and runtime variance while maintaining model performance and privacy.\nL146: ## III Floe: Fine-tuning Phase\nL147: \nL148: We now discuss how we leverage the derived principles to design a high-performance federated language model fine-tuning framework. Specifically, we first introduce the architecture and overall workflow of Floe. After that, we discuss each component in detail.\nL149: ### III-A System Overview\nL150: Figure cite112†6 depicts Floe’s federated fine-tuning architecture, which adopts a client-server design with specialized small language models (SLMs) deployed on edge devices (data owners). The process comprises four key stages: ➊ To minimize tuning overhead and communication costs, LoRA modules are used as the collaborative fine-tuning component.\nL151: ➋ Heterogeneity Adaptation: Each device freezes its SLM parameters and dynamically adjusts its LoRA trainer within local memory/latency constraints to accommodate hardware and runtime variance. ➌ Model Upload: After local training, devices upload their personalized LoRA modules (encoding task-specific adaptations) to the central server.\nL152: ➍ Hierarchical Aggregation: The server clusters LoRA modules based on embedding similarity, and aggregates them via a task-specific aggregator to update the global LoRA module.\nL153: ### III-B Heterogeneity-aware LoRA Trainer\nL154: The participating devices in federated fine-tuning often exhibit heterogeneous hardware configurations (e.g., varying compute capabilities and memory budgets) and runtime variances. Consequently, a uniform configuration cannot efficiently accommodate heterogeneous devices. While 1B-3B SLMs are classified as ’small’ relative to cloud LLMs, performing FFT on them remains infeasible for edge hardware due to excessive memory overheads.\nL155: Aligning with LoRA’s resource-dependent design philosophy, we employ a heterogeneity-aware LoRA trainer to bridge this gap, ensuring feasibility and maximizing participation across constrained edge devices. In vanilla LoRA, low-rank matrices A (initialized with Kaiming Uniform [cite120†66 ]) and B (zero-initialized) are sequentially applied to each layer to adapt residual weights. The forward computation is defined as:\nL156:  | $$y\\prime=y+\\Delta y=Wx+\\Delta Wx=Wx+BAx,$$  |  | (1)\nL157: where $y\\in\\mathbb{R}~\\textsuperscript{d}$ is the output and $x\\in\\mathbb{R}~\\textsuperscript{k}$ denote the input. $B\\in\\mathbb{R}~\\textsuperscript{d\\texttimes r},A\\in\\mathbb{R}~\\textsuperscript{r\\texttimes k}$ with $r\\ll min(d,k)$. $W$ represents the frozen model parameter. To address device heterogeneity, we dynamically configure LoRA ranks ($r$) for each device.\nL158: Given $N$ devices, we define a set of LoRA modules $\\Phi=\\{\\phi_{1},\\phi_{2},\\dots,\\phi_{N}\\}$, where each $\\phi_{i}$ is trained on local data. For device $i$, the LoRA rank $r_{i}$ is determined by:\nL159:  | $$\\left\\{\\begin{array}[]{l}A_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r_{i}\\times k}\\\\\nL160: B_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times r_{i}},\\end{array}\\right.$$  |  | (2)\nL161: subject to two constraints: 1) Memory Budget: $M_{r,i}\\leq\\mathcal{B}_{i}$ (device $i$’s memory capacity). 2) Latency Bound: Predicted training latency $t_{r,i}\\leq\\mathcal{T}$ (global round deadline to mitigate straggler effects [cite116†62 , cite103†54 ]). The function PredictLatency(r) is implemented as a look-up table (LUT) based on the offline profiling data.\nL162: This allows the trainer to directly retrieve the pre-measured training duration for the specific device and rank, ensuring precise deadline compliance. To intuitively illustrate the dynamic rank adaptation, we present the selection decision boundaries in Figure cite121†7 . This adaptive design ensures efficient resource utilization while maintaining training progress synchronization across heterogeneous edge devices.\nL163: It is orthogonal to compression-like methods, enabling further reduction in additional parameters and computation overhead. To regulate local data leakage, Floe can employ configurable Local Differential Privacy [cite122†67 , cite123†68 , cite124†69 ]. Gaussian noise is injected into the aggregated updates to satisfy differential privacy. The perturbed gradient is given by $\\tilde{g}=\\bar{g}+\\mathcal{N}(0,\\sigma_{noise}^{2}C^{2}\\mathbf{I})$, where $\\mathcal{N}$ denotes the Gaussian distribution.\nL164: Here, $C$ is the gradient clipping threshold used to bound the sensitivity, $\\sigma_{noise}$ is the noise multiplier, and $\\mathbf{I}$ is the identity matrix, ensuring independent noise injection across parameters. This formulation follows the standard DP-SGD protocol [cite122†67 , cite123†68 ]. Higher noise ($\\sigma$) yields stronger privacy but requires budget tuning to mitigate accuracy loss.\nL165: cite125†Image: Refer to caption Fig. 7: Illustration of Heterogeneity-aware Rank Selection. Algorithm 1 Heterogeneity-Aware Rank Selection\nL166: \nL167: 1:  Input: $\\mathcal{R}_{set}$: Available LoRA ranks; $\\mathcal{T}$: Global latency deadline; $\\text{GetAvailableMemory}()$: Gets current free memory; $\\text{PredictMemory}(r)$: Predicts memory use for rank $r$; $\\text{PredictLatency}(r)$: Predicts latency for rank $r$.\nL168: \nL169: 2:  Output: $r_{selected}$: The optimal LoRA rank for the current round.\nL170: \nL171: 3:  Function SELECT_RANK()\nL172: 4:  $r_{selected}\\leftarrow None$\nL173: \nL174: 5:  $\\mathcal{B}_{i}\\leftarrow\\text{GetAvailableMemory}()$\nL175: \nL176: 6:  for $r$ in $sorted(\\mathcal{R}_{set},\\text{reverse}=\\text{true})$ do\nL177: \nL178: 7:   $M_{r,i}\\leftarrow\\text{PredictMemory}(r)$\nL179: \nL180: 8:   /* Stage 1: check memory constraint */\nL181: \nL182: 9:   if $M_{r,i}\\leq\\mathcal{B}_{i}$ then\nL183: \nL184: 10:    $t_{r,i}\\leftarrow\\text{PredictLatency}(r)$\nL185: \nL186: 11:    /* Stage 2: check latency constraint */\nL187: \nL188: 12:    if $t_{r,i}\\leq\\mathcal{T}$ then\nL189: \nL190: 13:     $r_{selected}\\leftarrow r$\nL191: 14:     return $r_{selected}$\nL192: \nL193: 15:    end if\nL194: \nL195: 16:   end if\nL196: \nL197: 17:  end for\nL198: \nL199: 18:  return $r_{selected}$\nL200: \nL201: 19:  End Function\nL202: ### III-C Task-specific Router/Aggregator\nL203: To address cross-task heterogeneity under privacy and resource constraints, we design a task-specific LoRA aggregation strategy that extends beyond naive averaging. Each LoRA module $\\phi_{i}$ is first projected into a task-aware embedding space using a domain-conditioned encoder $E(\\phi_{i})$, capturing both its adaptation semantics and fine-tuning dynamics.\nL204: This embedding reflects not only parameter updates but also behavioral traits relevant to downstream generation, which are critical for privacy-sensitive, personalized tasks executed on edge devices. We then compute cosine similarities between embeddings to quantify functional divergence and form semantically coherent clusters:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - intuit-ai-research/REMem · GitHub (https://github.com/intuit-ai-research/REMem)\nciteturn31907view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/intuit-ai-research/REMem\",\"lineno\":220}); Tot