|    |\nL208: cite93†main.py | cite93†main.py |    |\nL209: cite94†pyproject.toml | cite94†pyproject.toml |    |\nL210: cite95†requirements.txt | cite95†requirements.txt |    |\nL211: cite96†requirements_nvemb.txt | cite96†requirements_nvemb.txt |    |\nL212: cite97†setup.py | cite97†setup.py |    |\nL213: [Button: View all files]\nL214: ## Repository files navigation\nL215: \nL216:   *   * cite98†README L217:   * cite98†Code of conduct L218:   * cite98†Contributing L219:   * cite98†Apache-2.0 license L220: \nL221: More items\nL222: \nL223: # REMem\nL224: ## What is REMem?\nL225: \nL226: REMem (Reasoning with Episodic Memory) is a retrieval-augmented generation system that organizes documents into a hybrid memory graph of entities, facts, and episodic gist traces. It combines dense retrieval with graph-based exploration to answer complex, multi-hop, and temporal questions over long-form text. See our cite99†ICLR 2026 paper†www.arxiv.org for details.\nL227: \nL228: ## Setup\nL229: ### Installation\nL230: \nL231: Install the package in editable mode with dependencies:\nL232: \nL233:     # Clone the repository\nL234:     git clone https://github.com/intuit-ai-research/REMem.git\nL235:     cd REMem\nL236: \nL237:     # Install in editable mode\nL238:     pip install -e .\nL239: \nL240: Or install from `requirements.txt`:\nL241: \nL242:     pip install -r requirements.txt\nL243:     pip install -e .\nL244: \nL245: Requires Python 3.10+.\nL246: ## Quick start\nL247: \nL248:     from remem.remem import ReMem\nL249:     from remem.utils.config_utils import BaseConfig\nL250: \nL251:     config = BaseConfig(\nL252:         dataset=\"sample\",\nL253:         extract_method=\"episodic_gist\",\nL254:         llm_name=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\nL255:         embedding_model_name=\"nvidia/NV-Embed-v2\",\nL256:     )\nL257: \nL258:     rag = ReMem(global_config=config)\nL259: \nL260:     docs = [\nL261:         \"Alan Turing proposed the Turing Test in 1950.\",\nL262:         \"Grace Hopper pioneered COBOL and popularized the term 'debugging'.\",\nL263:     ]\nL264:     rag.index(docs)\nL265: \nL266:     solutions, responses, meta = rag.rag_for_qa(\nL267:         [\"Who proposed the Turing Test?\", \"Who worked on COBOL?\"]\nL268:     )\nL269:     for s in solutions:\nL270:         print(s.question, \"->\", s.answer)\nL271: ## Running benchmarks\nL272: \nL273:     python main.py --dataset musique --llm_name gpt-4o-mini --embedding_name nvidia/NV-Embed-v2\nL274: \nL275: See `examples/` for dataset-specific scripts (MuSiQue, LoCoMo, LongMemEval, etc.) and `baselines/` for comparison methods.\nL276: ## Architecture\nL277: Layer  | What It Does  | Key Entry Points\nL278: --- | --- | ---\nL279: Orchestrator  | Coordinates everything  | `remem/remem.py` (`ReMem` class)\nL280: Preprocessing  | Chunking & text cleanup  | `graph/preprocessing/`\nL281: Extraction  | Text → entities / facts / episodic traces  | `information_extraction/`\nL282: Embeddings  | Encode passages, entities, facts, summaries  | `embedding_model/`, `embedding_store.py`\nL283: Graph Memory  | Build + persist hybrid graph  | `ReMem` (`add_*`, `augment_graph`)\nL284: Retrieval  | Combines dense, fact, and graph signals  | `rag_strategies/`\nL285: Reranking  | Optional DSPy filtering of facts  | `rerank.py`\nL286: Prompting  | Structured prompt templates per dataset  | `prompts/`\nL287: QA / Evaluation  | Generate answers + metrics  | `remem.py` + `evaluation/`\nL288: Agent  | Multi-step tool reasoning variants  | `agent/`\nL289: ### Processing pipeline\nL290:   1. Ingestion & Chunking — documents → normalized chunks\nL291:   2. Embedding Storage — passages inserted into `EmbeddingStore`\nL292:   3. Information Extraction — produces structured units (entities, triples, episodic traces, gists)\nL293:   4. Memory Graph Build\nL294:      * Facts: unit for embeddings and retrieval\nL295:      * Entities: unit for graph connections\nL296:      * Edges: gists → facts, entities ↔ entities\nL297:   5. Retrieval and QA\nL298:      * Initial retrieval via semantic/lexical search for gists and facts\nL299:      * Graph exploration: `find_entity_contexts`/`find_gist_contexts`\nL300:      * Answer generation\nL301:   6. Evaluation\nL302: ## Extraction methods\nL303: \nL304: Set `extract_method` in `BaseConfig`:\nL305: \nL306:   * `openie` — entity + triple extraction (lean & fast)\nL307:   * `episodic` — episodic fact extraction\nL308:   * `episodic_gist` — adds paraphrased gist memories for associative recall\nL309:   * `temporal` — emphasizes temporal anchors for time-sensitive QA\nL310: \nL311: Each mode influences which embedding stores exist, what graph nodes are created, and how retrieval seeds PageRank.\nL312: ## Project structure\nL313: \nL314:     `src/remem/\nL315:       remem.py                  # orchestrator (index, retrieve, rag_for_qa)\nL316:       embedding_store.py        # local embedding store\nL317:       embedding_model/          # embedding backends (OpenAI, NV-Embed-v2, GritLM, Qwen3)\nL318:       llm/                      # LLM backends (OpenAI API, Azure, vLLM offline)\nL319:       information_extraction/   # extraction methods\nL320:       rag_strategies/           # retrieval strategies\nL321:       graph/                    # preprocessing, graph construction\nL322:       prompts/                  # prompt templates\nL323:       rerank.py                 # DSPy-based fact filtering (optional)\nL324:       evaluation/               # metrics (EM, F1, BLEU, NDCG, LLM-judge)\nL325:       agent/                    # multi-step reasoning variants\nL326:       utils/                    # config, hashing, datetime, misc\nL327:     examples/                   # benchmark scripts\nL328:     baselines/                  # comparison baselines\nL329:     reproduce/                  # datasets and reproducibility\nL330:     `\nL331: ### Key files\nL332: \nL333: File  | Why It Matters\nL334: --- | ---\nL335: `remem.py`  | Unified API: indexing, retrieval, QA, evaluation\nL336: `embedding_store.py`  | Stores & reuses embeddings; enables incremental runs\nL337: `rag_strategies/*`  | Swap retrieval logic without rewriting orchestration\nL338: `information_extraction/*`  | Controls what memory units get stored\nL339: `prompts/prompt_template_manager.py`  | Centralizes templates for extraction & QA\nL340: `utils/config_utils.py`  | Single config object flows through all components\nL341: ## Customization\nL342: You Want To…  | Touch\nL343: --- | ---\nL344: Add a new embedding backend  | Add class in `embedding_model/` + register in `__init__.py`\nL345: Change chunking rules  | Implement new preprocessor in `graph/preprocessing/`\nL346: Introduce new extraction style  | Add module under `information_extraction/` + update factory in `remem.py`\nL347: Modify ranking blend  | Edit / extend a strategy in `rag_strategies/`\nL348: Add new prompt template  | Drop file in `prompts/templates/` and reference by name\nL349: Plug in new metric  | Implement under `evaluation/` and include in `remem.py:get_evaluators`\nL350: Use multi-step reasoning  | Explore `agent/` strategies (fixed vs flexible tools)\nL351: ## Citation\nL352: \nL353: If you use REMem in your research, please cite our paper:\nL354: \nL355:     @inproceedings{shu2026remem,\nL356:       title={REMem: Reasoning with Episodic Memory in Language Agents},\nL357:       author={Shu, Yiheng and Jonnalagedda, Saisri Padmaja and Gao, Xiang and Guti{\\'e}rrez, Bernal Jim{\\'e}nez and Qi, Weijian and Das, Kamalika and Sun, Huan and Su, Yu},\nL358:       booktitle={The Thirteenth International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - ybwang119/ASCL: [ICML 2026] Official code for ICML 2026 paper: Mitigating the Safety–Utility Trade-off in LLM Alignment via Adaptive Safe Context Learning · GitHub (https://github.com/ybwang119/ASCL)\nciteturn31907view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/ybwang119/ASCL\",\"lineno\":210}); Total lines: 289\nL120:   * cite62†Code L121:   * cite64†Issues 1 L122:   * cite65†Pull requests 0 L123:   * cite66†Actions L124:   * cite67†Projects L125:   * cite68†Security and quality 0 L126:   * cite69†Insights L127: \nL128: Additional navigation options\nL129: \nL130: main\nL131: \nL132: cite70†Branches cite71†Tags L133: \nL134: [Input: Go to file]\nL135: \nL136: Go to file\nL137: \nL138: Code\nL139: \nL140: Open more actions menu\nL141: ## Latest commit\nL142: \nL143: \nL144: \nL145: ## History\nL146: \nL147: 2 Commits\nL148: ## Folders and files\nL149: \nL150: Name  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\nL151: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL152: cite72†benchmark | cite72†benchmark |    |\nL153: cite73†config | cite73†config |    |\nL154: cite74†knowledge_base | cite74†knowledge_base |    |\nL155: cite75†src | cite75†src |    |\nL156: cite76†tools | cite76†tools |    |\nL157: cite77†train | cite77†train |    |\nL158: cite78†.gitignore | cite78†.gitignore |    |\nL159: cite79†readme.md | cite79†readme.md |    |\nL160: cite80†requirements.txt | cite80†requirements.txt |    |\nL161: ## Repository files navigation\nL162: \nL163:   *   * cite81†README L164: \nL165: More items\nL166: # Mitigating the Safety–Utility Trade-off in LLM Alignment via Adaptive Safe Context Learning\nL167: \nL168: This repo contains the official code for our ICML 2026 paper cite82†Mitigating the Safety–Utility Trade-off in LLM Alignment via Adaptive Safe Context Learning†arxiv.org .\nL169: \nL170: > Note on releases. Due to some constraints, we are unable to open-source the trained model checkpoints. However, we do release the training data we collected, available under `train/training_data/`.\nL171: ## Requirements\nL172: \nL173: We recommend installing the official veRL Docker environment directly, which already ships the compatible versions of torch, vLLM, flash-attn, Ray, etc. See the cite83†veRL installation docs†verl.readthedocs.io for the available images.\nL174: \nL175: After setting up the veRL environment, install the remaining project dependencies:\nL176: \nL177:     pip install -r requirements.txt\nL178: Alternatively, you can create a conda environment from scratch, but matching the veRL/vLLM/flash-attn versions manually can be error-prone:\nL179: \nL180:     conda create -n ascl python=3.10 -y\nL181:     conda activate ascl\nL182:     pip install -r requirements.txt\nL183: ## 1. Enable RAG and MCP\nL184: \nL185: The safety agent relies on two services: a RAG service for safety-policy retrieval and an MCP server that exposes the retrieval tool to the model. Both are configured in `config/rag_config.yaml` (knowledge base, ports, embedding model).\nL186: \nL187: Start both services with a single script:\nL188: \nL189:     cd src\nL190:     ./start_safety_agent.sh\nL191: \nL192: This launches:\nL193: \nL194:   * RAG retrieval service (default `http://localhost:8002`)\nL195:   * MCP tool server (port from `config/rag_config.yaml`)\nL196: Stop the services with the dedicated script:\nL197: \nL198:     cd src\nL199:     ./stop_all.sh          # stop Agent, MCP, and RAG services\nL200:     ./stop_all.sh --vllm   # also stop the vLLM model services\nL201: ## 2. Training\nL202: \nL203: All training-related settings (data paths, model path, batch size, epochs, checkpoint dir, etc.) are configured in the YAML files under `train/training_config/`. Training data is stored as Parquet under `train/training_data/parquet/`.\nL204: \nL205: ### SFT (Supervised Fine-tuning)\nL206: \nL207: Edit the relevant config in `train/training_config/` and set `CONFIG_NAME` in `train/run_sft.sh` (e.g. `config_sft_verl_star1`, `config_sft_verl_safechain`, `config_sft`), then run:\nL208: \nL209:     cd train\nL210:     ./run_sft.sh\nL211: ### RL (GRPO / IFPO)\nL212: \nL213: RL training relies on both the MCP server (see step 1) and a reward model cluster (safety scoring + hallucination detection). Start the reward models first:\nL214: \nL215:     python tools/start_reward_model_cluster.py\nL216: \nL217: This serves the safety reward model at `http://localhost:8010` and the hallucination model at `http://localhost:8050` (paths/ports configurable via env vars and the constants at the top of the script).\nL218: Then configure the RL settings in the YAML (e.g. `config_ifpo`, `config_grpo`), set `CONFIG_NAME` in `train/run_rl_agent.sh`, and run:\nL219: \nL220:     cd train\nL221:     ./run_rl_agent.sh\nL222: \nL223: Checkpoints are written to `trainer.default_local_dir` defined in the config.\nL224: ## 3. Evaluation\nL225: \nL226: Evaluation uses the modular benchmark in `benchmark/`. The model and dataset settings (model paths, API endpoints, datasets, eval model, sampling, etc.) are all configured in the YAML files `benchmark/configs/model_config.yaml` and `benchmark/configs/dataset_config.yaml`.\nL227: Run generation + evaluation through the unified entry point:\nL228: \nL229:     cd benchmark\nL230: \nL231:     # Generate + evaluate\nL232:     python exp_all.py --mode both \\\nL233:         --models <your-model> \\\nL234:         --datasets strongreject wildjailbreak\nL235: \nL236:     # Generation only\nL237:     python exp_all.py --mode gen --models <your-model> --datasets strongreject\nL238: \nL239:     # Evaluation only\nL240:     python exp_all.py --mode eval --models <your-model> --datasets strongreject\nL241: After the run finishes, view the aggregated metrics (safety rate, refusal rate, accuracy, etc.) with `calculate_results.py`:\nL242: \nL243:     python calculate_results.py --result_dir results\nL244: \nL245: See `benchmark/readme.md` for detailed options (GPT-4o / Llama-Guard / Qwen-3-Guard evaluators, GPU management, etc.).\nL246: ## Citation\nL247: \nL248:     @inproceedings{wang2026mitigating,\nL249:       title={Mitigating the Safety-utility Trade-off in LLM Alignment via Adaptive Safe Context Learning},\nL250:       author={Wang, Yanbo and Wang, Minzheng and Liang, Jian and Wang, Lu and Yu, Yongcan and He, Ran},\nL251:       booktitle={Proc. ICML},\nL252:       year={2026}\nL253:     }\nL254: ## About\nL255: \nL256: [ICML 2026] Official code for ICML 2026 paper: Mitigating the Safety–Utility Trade-off in LLM Alignment via Adaptive Safe Context Learning\nL257: \nL258: cite82†arxiv.org/abs/2602.13562†arxiv.org L259: \nL260: ### Resources\nL261: \nL262: cite84†Readme L263: \nL264: cite85†Activity L265: \nL266: ### Stars\nL267: \nL268: 4 stars\nL269: \nL270: ### Watchers\nL271: \nL272: 0 watching\nL273: \nL274: ### Forks\nL275: \nL276: cite86†0 forks L277: \nL278: cite87†Report repository L279: \nL280: ## Releases\nL281: \nL282: ## Packages\nL283: \nL284: ## Contributors\nL285: \nL286: ## Languages\nL287: \nL288: You can’t perform that action at this time.\n"
